The problem asks us to find the number of undirected connected simple graphs with $N$ vertices (labeled 1 to $N$) and $M$ edges such that the number of vertices with even shortest distance from vertex 1 is equal to the number of vertices with odd shortest distance from vertex 1. We need to output this count modulo $P$ for all $M$ from $N-1$ to $\frac{N(N-1)}{2}$.
$N$ is an even integer, $2 \le N \le 30$. $P$ is a prime, $10^8 \le P \le 10^9$.

Let $d(v)$ be the shortest distance from vertex 1 to vertex $v$ in the graph.
The condition is that $|\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}| = |\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}|$.
Since there are $N$ vertices in total, and $N$ is even, this condition implies that exactly $N/2$ vertices have even distance and $N/2$ vertices have odd distance.
Note that $d(1) = 0$, which is even. So vertex 1 is always counted in the even set.
Thus, we need exactly $N/2 - 1$ other vertices to have even distance, and $N/2$ vertices to have odd distance.

Let $S_{even} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}$ and $S_{odd} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}$.
We require $|S_{even}| = |S_{odd}| = N/2$.
Since $1 \in S_{even}$, we need to choose $N/2 - 1$ vertices from $\{2, \dots, N\}$ to be in $S_{even}$. The remaining $N/2$ vertices will be in $S_{odd}$.
Let $A = S_{even} \setminus \{1\}$ and $B = S_{odd}$. Then $A \cup B = \{2, \dots, N\}$, $A \cap B = \emptyset$, $|A| = N/2 - 1$, $|B| = N/2$.
The condition on distances implies that for any $u \in S_{even}$ and $v \in S_{odd}$, the distance $d(u, v)$ must be odd. For any $u, v \in S_{even}$, $d(u, v)$ must be even. For any $u, v \in S_{odd}$, $d(u, v)$ must be even.
Wait, this is a necessary condition derived from the triangle inequality $d(u, v) \le d(u, 1) + d(1, v)$.
Specifically, if $u, v \in S_{even}$, then $d(u, 1)$ and $d(v, 1)$ are even. $d(u, v) \le d(u, 1) + d(1, v) = \text{even} + \text{even} = \text{even}$.
If $u, v \in S_{odd}$, then $d(u, 1)$ and $d(v, 1)$ are odd. $d(u, v) \le d(u, 1) + d(1, v) = \text{odd} + \text{odd} = \text{even}$.
If $u \in S_{even}, v \in S_{odd}$, then $d(u, 1)$ is even, $d(v, 1)$ is odd. $d(u, v) \le d(u, 1) + d(1, v) = \text{even} + \text{odd} = \text{odd}$.
Also, $d(u, v) \ge |d(u, 1) - d(v, 1)|$.
If $u, v \in S_{even}$, $|d(u, 1) - d(v, 1)|$ is even. So $d(u, v)$ must be even.
If $u, v \in S_{odd}$, $|d(u, 1) - d(v, 1)|$ is even. So $d(u, v)$ must be even.
If $u \in S_{even}, v \in S_{odd}$, $|d(u, 1) - d(v, 1)|$ is odd. So $d(u, v)$ must be odd.
So the condition is equivalent to is_correct=False justification=Code is incorrect.
